, facing than to any other in connection with the construction of these highways; therefore, it is of the greatest importance, in the event this amendment carries, that the highway advisory board consist largely of men whose training and experience will enable them to aid, by their advice, the state engineer in properly solving the road surfacing and other kindred problems. It is necessary that they be of such standing in the state as to insure confidence among the people that this fund is being honestly and efficiently expended for the purpose it was intended. They will have all the authority necessary to do this; authority to inspect the work, to examine the accounts and to make public their findings. Publicity is the best and only necessary safeguard to put around the expenditure of public funds. It protects both the public and the honest official.

 The chief advantage to be derived by the sale of bonds for the construction of these roads are:

 First, the people can realize in a few years the benefits of a well-constructed system of state highways that it will take more than 20 years to complete with our present state road appropriations;

 Second, the cost of construction of the proposed highways will be considerably less if carried on continuously to completion than if constructed piece by piece, year by year, in the fourteen different counties as is now being done;

 Third, that the cost of maintenance for the same reason will be less, for a large continuous mileage can be more efficiently and economically maintained than can disconnected units;

 Fourth, that as posterity benefits by the development of our resources, posterity will share the burden;

 Fifth, that the increase in the value of lands and in the volume of business will be immediate and substantial and will probably be sufficient to meet the increased tax rate necessary to pay the interest on, and to retire, the bonds;

 Sixth, that improved roads will result in large savings in the cost of transportation by reason of lighter grades and more stable wearing surface decreasing the expense of operation of all vehicles and effecting a great economy of time. The saving in the item of wear and tear upon vehicles using the state highways will alone justify the expenditure;

 Seventh, this is the beginning of the era of overland travel and the tourists who will be attracted to the state by reason of a well-constructed system of state highways will be in such great number that should one submit even a moderate estimate the unkind would accuse him of being either a dreamer or inspired by ulterior motives. The amount of revenue that will be derived from such a source can be appreciated by all who have visited our sister state on the west; in addition to the pecuniary profits, these travellers will furnish a medium of advertising the undeveloped resources of the state, the value of which is beyond measure.

 To initiate constitutional amendment requires petitions signed by 3243 qualified electors, which have to be filed in the office of the secretary of state before midnight of July 2.

 I would appreciate very much your opinion as to the merits of the proposed amendment

 Yours, very truly,

 LAMAR COBB,

 Arizona State Engineer.

